Yes. The useful version is not merely “AI, is there a shark tooth in this picture?” It should behave more like visual radar: systematically sweep the image, construct a probability field, and tell you whether the patch deserves your eyes.

I’d call it ToothDAR.

Your photo is actually a perfect stress test: hundreds of shell fragments produce edges, triangles, gray/black patches, and reflections. A model that shrinks the entire image and looks once will lose the tiny objects we care about. The fundamental trick is high-resolution recursive scanning.

The core algorithm

Take the full-resolution image I, divide it into overlapping tiles, and inspect each tile at several magnifications.

PHOTO
 ↓
quality / glare / blur check
 ↓
split into overlapping tiles
 ↓
scan every tile at 1×, 2×, 4× effective scale
 ↓
candidate detector
 ↓
tooth-vs-shell classifier
 ↓
shape + texture + geometry analysis
 ↓
merge overlapping detections
 ↓
TOOTH PROBABILITY MAP
 ↓
"Worth looking here?" score

Instead of returning only tooth / no tooth, it creates a field:

T(x,y)=P(\text{shark tooth at location }x,y)

You would see something analogous to a thermal camera:

red = high-confidence tooth candidate
orange = inspect this
yellow = weak possibility
nothing = probably not worth bending over for

The killer feature is then a second quantity:

\text{STOP SCORE}
=
P(\text{tooth})
\times
\text{estimated size}
\times
\text{visible completeness}
\times
\text{multi-view confidence}

So the phone could simply say:

SCAN RESULT: LOW YIELD
No strong candidates. Keep walking.

or:

SCAN RESULT: STOP
2 probable teeth detected.
Candidate A: 91% — likely 18–25 mm.
Candidate B: 67% — partially buried.

That saves you from studying every square inch yourself.

What the detector should actually learn

Don’t train it only on shark teeth. That creates a terrible false-positive machine.

Teach it the ecology of impostors:

* shark tooth
* broken oyster shell
* triangular clam fragment
* dark shell fragment
* pebble
* wood
* crab shell
* sea glass
* fossil fragment
* shadow
* leaf/debris

Then give the tooth detector some explicit morphology. It should look for converging crown edges, an apex, root shoulders/lobes, crown-root transition, bilateral organization, characteristic surface texture, and eventually serrations when there are enough pixels.

That gives us two independent judges:

\text{Tooth Score}
=
\text{Neural Recognition}
+
\text{Morphological Fit}
+
\text{Material Fit}
-
\text{Confounder Score}

They don’t have to agree. Their disagreement itself is useful information.

The really good version uses video

This is where your LiDAR analogy gets much closer.

Instead of photographing the ground, hold the phone 2–4 feet above it and make a slow sweep.

The phone uses:

camera + IMU + AR ground-plane tracking + optional phone LiDAR/depth

to construct a little map of the shell bed.

A genuine candidate should persist across several frames:

frame 17     possible triangle
frame 18     still there
frame 19     same geometry, slightly different viewpoint
frame 20     crown/root geometry becomes clearer
→ confidence rises dramatically

A reflection or accidental arrangement of three shell fragments often disappears when the viewing angle changes.

That’s essentially synthetic visual parallax. We make the environment tell us whether the shape is a real object.

On phones with depth sensing, depth isn’t what identifies the tooth—but it helps determine its physical size, orientation, how buried it is, and whether several apparent fragments actually lie on one coherent surface.

And then make it learn you

Every time it highlights something, you tap:

TOOTH ✓
or
NOT A TOOTH ×

And whenever you actually find a tooth that it missed, photograph it before picking it up and hit:

MISSED ONE

Those misses are gold.

The system gradually learns the exact beaches, fossil colors, shell beds, sizes, and visual signatures you encounter. This is active learning rather than building one supposedly universal detector.

Eventually your personal version could become considerably better than a generic shark-tooth model.

There should actually be three modes

Quick Scan — snap one picture. Takes perhaps a second or two and answers, “Anything worth inspecting?”

Sweep Scan — move the camera slowly over a patch. Builds the ToothDAR probability map and finds tiny/partially concealed specimens.

Live Hunt — walk with the camera pointed down. Candidate boxes remain anchored to the ground. Phone vibrates when a high-confidence target enters the field.

That last one is basically:

Pokémon Go for shark teeth.

You could even hear a little ping whose pitch increases with confidence.

⸻

And there is an important second-order feature I think you’re really after.

Don’t only detect individual teeth.

Have the system learn productive ground.

The network can compute something like:

Y = f(
shell\ density,\;
fragment\ size,\;
sediment,\;
fossil\ debris,\;
waterline,\;
sorting,\;
previous\ finds
)

So eventually you point it at a patch and it tells you:

TOOTH PRESENT: uncertain
HABITAT/YIELD SCORE: 87/100
This substrate resembles locations where you’ve found teeth before.

Now you’ve moved from object detection to terrain prediction.

That is potentially much more powerful because a hidden tooth may be impossible to see—but the environment that concentrates teeth can still be recognizable.

And your particular photo demonstrates why I’d build it this way: it is a high-clutter, high-entropy shell bed with thousands of tooth-like edges. I don’t see an obvious high-confidence tooth jumping out at me in the full frame, but that’s exactly the scenario where the image should be tiled into dozens of high-resolution micro-searches instead of trusting a whole-image glance.

The MVP is surprisingly attainable: high-res tiled detector → heatmap → candidate zooms → yes/no feedback. Then we add the video sweep, AR persistence, depth, personal learning, and finally predictive tooth terrain.

That last transition is the interesting one: we stop asking “Where is the tooth?” and start asking “What visual conditions generate a high posterior probability that this ground is worth searching?” That’s your LiDAR/rangefinder pattern applied correctly.